A packaged app (MSIX: Store PowerShell, winget, any App Execution Alias) is created in its package's own job, one per
package and user, which every running process of the package shares. 0004's PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST puts the
child into the pane's job as it is created, so CreateProcessW is refused with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED whenever another
process of the package already runs: the package's job is then neither empty nor in the hierarchy of the pane's. With
none running, the start succeeds but nests the package's job under that pane's, and every later start of the package
fails the same way, inside ngterm or outside it, until the pane closes. So the first pane of Store PowerShell worked and
the next stayed empty (nickalie/ngterm#19).

With a job, the child is now started suspended (CREATE_SUSPENDED), assigned with AssignProcessToJobObject, then
resumed; the job list is gone, and with it 0004's `ProcThreadAttributeList::set_job_list` and its attribute constant.
The empty pane job then nests under the package's job, and panes and other starts of the package stay independent. An
unpackaged child runs nothing before it is in the job, so it and every process it starts stay in the job as before.
A packaged child's descendants would leave its package's job, and so the pane's, by default: the child is also given
PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_DESKTOP_APP_POLICY with PROCESS_CREATION_DESKTOP_APP_BREAKAWAY_DISABLE_PROCESS_TREE
(`ProcThreadAttributeList::set_desktop_app_policy`, Windows 10 1703 and newer, older than ConPTY), so they stay in the
job, and the pane's foreground and its tree kill reach them. They then run inside the package's runtime: its identity,
and the file-system and registry virtualization its manifest asks for (PowerShell's turns both off). A different
packaged app started from the pane still activates in its own package's job and leaves the pane's. For an unpackaged
child the policy changes nothing. A failed assignment or resume terminates the suspended child, which no job would
end, and returns the step's `io::Error` under a context that names the command line, as 0009 does for CreateProcessW;
ngterm-pty reads it to name the program as written (`crates/ngterm-pty/src/windows/mod.rs`). The assignment needs
winapi's `jobapi2`. Without a job (the slave's `spawn_command`) the start is unchanged.

ngterm's own. Remove with 0004, when portable-pty takes a job for the spawn and assigns it before the child runs. Its
tests: ngterm-pty's `a_job_that_refuses_the_child_leaves_no_process_behind` and
`a_packaged_app_starts_while_another_process_of_its_package_runs`.

